Consider the following $100$ bins of $10$ items each:
\begin{align*} &\{1,2,3,\ldots,10\}, \\ &\{11,12,13,\ldots,20\},\\ &\{21,22,23,\ldots,30\},\\ &\vdots\\ &\{991,992,993,\ldots,1000\}. \end{align*}
How many of these bins contain exactly two multiples of $7$?

Consider the following $100$ sets of $10$ elements each:
\begin{align*} &\{1,2,3,\ldots,10\}, \\ &\{11,12,13,\ldots,20\},\\ &\{21,22,23,\ldots,30\},\\ &\vdots\\ &\{991,992,993,\ldots,1000\}. \end{align*}
A multiple of a positive integer is the product of that integer and another integer. How many of these sets contain exactly two multiples of $7$?

A warehouse stores crates in $100$ batches of $10$ serial numbers each:
\begin{align*} &\{1,2,3,\ldots,10\}, \\ &\{11,12,13,\ldots,20\},\\ &\{21,22,23,\ldots,30\},\\ &\vdots\\ &\{991,992,993,\ldots,1000\}. \end{align*}
How many of these batches contain exactly two serial numbers that are multiples of $7$?

